// SOS M3 unit 5: A BOUND LINE COMES BACK MID-LIFE (sawos design 7 D-1; spec
// §9).
//
// **THIS PROGRAM COULD NOT HAVE SURVIVED YESTERDAY.** Until free-on-zero, an
// Interrupt's slot came back at its process's teardown and at no other moment,
// so the line it held stayed bound for as long as the process lived — and a
// second bind of the same line was a `LineBound` FAULT, which ends the process.
// The transcript is therefore the whole claim:
//
//   SOS irqunbind: bound line 5
//   SOS irqunbind: rebound=1              <- the line was genuinely returned
//   SOS: process teardown ... interrupts={one} ...
//
// **AND THE MASK IS THE PART THAT IS NOT BOOKKEEPING.** Freeing the slot is
// half of it; the other half is that the kernel takes the LINE back, exactly as
// the teardown does — §9's handover run backwards. A device the dropping
// process was driving may still be asserting, and an unmasked line with no
// object behind it would be taken, reported and masked once per fire forever.
// That half is not directly observable from userspace, and the rebind is what
// stands in for it: `interrupt_bind` UNMASKS on success, so a line that came
// back masked is a line the second bind could arm.
//
// `interrupts={one}` in the teardown is the small statement beside the big one:
// this program bound the same line twice and the kernel is holding exactly one
// Interrupt when it ends. Two would mean the first was never reclaimed — and it
// could not be two anyway, because the second bind would have been the fault.
//
// THE RELEASE IS SPELLED AS A DROP, which is the typed layer's only spelling
// for it (design 3 D-5): `let _ = move first` consumes the wrapper and its
// deinit issues the syscall. Nothing here names a handle word.

import sos.{System, Process, Interrupt}

/// A line both machines wire and neither asserts unasked — `irq_early_ack`'s
/// own choice, and for the same reason: nothing can fire between the bind and
/// the release, so the sequence below is forced rather than raced.
static QUIET_LINE: UInt = 5

static EXIT_OK: UInt = 0
static EXIT_FAILED: UInt = 1

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    let proc = try system.process_self() catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS irqunbind: no process handle\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    let first = try proc.interrupt_bind(line: QUIET_LINE) catch {
        print("SOS irqunbind: first bind failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    print("SOS irqunbind: bound line {}\n", QUIET_LINE)

    // **THE RELEASE.** This is the only handle onto the object, so the count
    // reaches zero inside this line: the kernel masks the line and the slot goes
    // back to the slab, both before the next statement runs.
    let _ = move first

    // **AND THE PROOF.** A line still bound answers `LineBound`, which is a
    // FAULT and would end this process without printing anything further. The
    // line below existing in the transcript at all is the claim.
    let second = try proc.interrupt_bind(line: QUIET_LINE) catch {
        print("SOS irqunbind: rebind refused: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS irqunbind: rebound=1\n")

    // The second Interrupt is still held here — nothing consumes `second`, and
    // `exit` does not return — so the teardown reclaims exactly one, which is
    // what its count says. EXIT rather than SHUTDOWN, so that line is printed
    // at all: `shutdown` stops the machine with nothing said.
    let _ = proc.exit(EXIT_OK)
    let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
}
